WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
(music)

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:07.080
- This traditional water mill can grind

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.139
over 550 pounds of flour in a single day.

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:13.139
(music)

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.203
This is the Lurgashall Watermill

00:00:18.203 --> 00:00:22.320
of Weald and Downland Museum
in Singleton, West Sussex.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.909
- The way we make the flour
is exactly the same way

00:00:24.909 --> 00:00:29.406
as it's been made for generations.

00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:30.989
The wheat goes in at the top,

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:33.084
the flour comes out of the bottom.

00:00:33.084 --> 00:00:34.972
We don't add anything in at all.

00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:36.755
We don't take anything out.

00:00:36.755 --> 00:00:37.805
(music)

00:00:38.582 --> 00:00:41.585
- [Narrator] The Lurgashall
Mill dates the 17th century,

00:00:41.585 --> 00:00:44.619
but despite being over 400 years old,

00:00:44.619 --> 00:00:47.769
it hasn't always been
in the same location.

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:50.158
Its original site was
the Lurgashall village

00:00:50.158 --> 00:00:53.491
in West Sussex, which it is named after.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.289
The original mill had two
water wheels in tandem,

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:01.869
each wheel driving its own
independent set of machinery.

00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:05.390
With time, only the tail
water wheel remained in use

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.900
and the cast-iron wheel
replaced the wooden one.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.630
The Lurgashall Mill was incorporated

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.438
into the museum in 1973.

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:16.118
The museum carried out restoration works

00:01:16.118 --> 00:01:18.798
of the machinery and the interior,

00:01:18.798 --> 00:01:21.370
but it was still lacking
something essential.

00:01:22.878 --> 00:01:27.300
- The problem was that on this
site we don't have a river.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.910
So, we had to engineer
a whole water system,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.928
dig out a big pond, and line it

00:01:33.928 --> 00:01:37.788
because underneath is
chalk, which absorbs water.

00:01:37.788 --> 00:01:41.348
And then, below the wheel
we need another pond

00:01:41.348 --> 00:01:43.078
to collect the water.

00:01:43.078 --> 00:01:45.927
That had to be dug out and lined.

00:01:45.927 --> 00:01:48.100
And because there's no
river, we have to pump

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.767
that water back up and reuse it.

00:01:51.758 --> 00:01:53.009
- [Narrator] Nowadays, the mill is used

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:56.790
for grinding corn, flour, and animal feed.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.156
It supplies to small bakers and farm shops

00:01:59.156 --> 00:02:01.573
around the Singleton village.

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:06.556
We followed a journey of a bag of wheat

00:02:06.556 --> 00:02:08.806
as it is milled into flour.

00:02:10.089 --> 00:02:12.619
The mill is structured over three floors.

00:02:12.619 --> 00:02:15.198
The top floor, where grain is fed from,

00:02:15.198 --> 00:02:17.340
the first floor, or stone floor,

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.318
where the grinding takes
place, and the ground floor,

00:02:20.318 --> 00:02:23.300
or spout floor, where a horizontal shaft

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.650
connects the water wheel to the machinery.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.390
- The mill's driven by water and so,

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:31.452
we need to have water
flowing over the waterwheel.

00:02:31.452 --> 00:02:32.321
We start the mill

00:02:32.321 --> 00:02:36.258
by taking a very large spanner
up to the sluice gates,

00:02:36.258 --> 00:02:38.441
which control where the water comes out

00:02:38.441 --> 00:02:42.130
of the big mill pond.
We open the sluice gate,

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.538
water then flows along a channel,

00:02:44.538 --> 00:02:48.812
at the back of the mill, until
it reaches the water wheel;

00:02:48.812 --> 00:02:51.860
and it starts to fill
up what we call buckets.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.660
They don't look like the bucket,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.561
that you would use for
cleaning your floors,

00:02:55.561 --> 00:03:00.241
something like that; but,
each slot contains water,

00:03:00.241 --> 00:03:02.618
and we need to fill about four of those,

00:03:02.618 --> 00:03:06.220
before the weight of the
water makes the wheel turn,

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.401
and then the water tops up the next one,

00:03:08.401 --> 00:03:12.830
and the next one so it
keeps turning continuously.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.289
- The mill is 12 feet in
diameter, when grinding,

00:03:16.289 --> 00:03:19.138
it turns at about six
revolutions per minute;

00:03:19.138 --> 00:03:22.830
each complete turn is powered
by 200 gallons of water.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.081
(music)

00:03:25.081 --> 00:03:28.580
- When we need to mill, we
take a bag into the mill,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:29.961
and we use a chain hoist,

00:03:29.961 --> 00:03:33.544
to lift it up to the
top floor of the mill;

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:43.098
we then open the bag,

00:03:43.098 --> 00:03:44.889
sometimes the string will pull,

00:03:44.889 --> 00:03:47.108
sometimes we have to use scissors,

00:03:47.108 --> 00:03:49.417
and we then poor it down the chute,

00:03:49.417 --> 00:03:51.769
into a hopper above the machinery.

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:53.769
(music)

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.420
- In this big barrel,
there are two stones,

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.380
just like these miniature ones,

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.209
the bottom one called the
bedstone that doesn't move,

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.750
the top one called the runnerstone,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.481
is turned by the machinery

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:18.481
and is the one that is
actually doing the grinding.

00:04:18.481 --> 00:04:21.158
- We can control the grade of the flour

00:04:21.158 --> 00:04:24.100
by the amount of grain that we put in,

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.989
and the distance apart of the stones,

00:04:26.989 --> 00:04:28.441
and everything is checked,

00:04:28.441 --> 00:04:30.420
by what is known as the rule of thumb.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.201
With no gauges, no machinery,

00:04:33.201 --> 00:04:36.590
we just use our thumb and our forefinger,

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.510
and if it feels right, it is right.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.340
(music)

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.298
- Overall the stones
rotate 13 and a half times,

00:04:43.298 --> 00:04:45.241
each rotation of the water wheel,

00:04:45.241 --> 00:04:49.177
at an average speed of 80
revolutions per minute.

00:04:49.177 --> 00:04:51.827
- I enjoy the fact that
when I come to the Museum,

00:04:51.836 --> 00:04:54.379
at the end of the day, I
can look at a pile of flour

00:04:54.379 --> 00:04:56.500
and say: "I helped to make that"

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.329
and because we're also a
museum, and we have visitors,

00:05:00.329 --> 00:05:03.898
its fascinating to talk to the visitors,

00:05:03.898 --> 00:05:06.062
and some of them tell me stories,

00:05:06.062 --> 00:05:08.644
about how they have
seen a mill being used,

00:05:08.644 --> 00:05:10.302
when they were children.

00:05:10.302 --> 00:05:14.244
You get people who come from
various parts of the world,

00:05:14.244 --> 00:05:16.444
telling us how it was used,

00:05:16.444 --> 00:05:18.284
one lady from Cambodia,

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:21.993
told me how to make
rice noodles using one.

00:05:21.993 --> 00:05:23.993
(music)

